; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; -------------------------------------------------------------------------
; RECONSTRUCTED -- not the upstream file.
;
; provenance.tier: reconstructed
; source-of-record: robosimstudio/tasks/tool_sort/suite.py
; upstream:         VAB-industrial/bddl_files/tool_sort/put_the_syringe_in_the_sharps_box.bddl
;
; The upstream .bddl files are NOT vendored here -- assets/PROVENANCE.md is
; explicit that the VAB-industrial BDDL content is transcribed into the suite
; as region literals and goal pairs, not copied as files. This reverses that
; transcription: every number below is read back out of the suite, which took
; them from the original to the digit.
;
; READ THE CIRCULARITY BEFORE TRUSTING A ROUND TRIP. A parser checked against
; this file is checked against our own transcription, not against upstream.
;
; One object, posted through a hole. The scene has no bins -- the syringe
; lies on the workbench and the box stands on it, because a 0.18 m box on
; a bin floor puts its hole above what reach the arm has left while
; holding a syringe on end. The bin bodies remain as the frames everything
; else is placed in. Containment is literal: the region is the box's
; cavity, so a syringe left on the lid is 0.17 m above it and does not
; count.
;
; This was `sharps_disposal/syringe` for a while -- forked out of tool_sort
; because posting one object through a sealed aperture read as a different
; skill from sorting a pile -- and merged back on 2026-09-02. The fork was
; 59% byte-identical to its parent and its solution differed by four lines
; (a class name and a register-function name): one goal, one export family,
; one cell. What it kept of its own is below: its profile, its records, a
; grasp offset in routine.py and a perception graph of its own.
; -------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (problem PUT_THE_SYRINGE_IN_THE_SHARPS_BOX)

  (:language pick up the syringe from the workbench and drop it through the
             hole in the top of the yellow sharps disposal box)

  ; What BDDL cannot say: which cell, which robot, which export. The profile
  ; supplies the first two for every VAB-industrial problem; the export is
  ; this variant's own, and names the dumped scene its poses come from.
  (:staging
    ; Not the suite's `vab_tool_sort@v2`, and the difference is one camera:
    ; `agentview@v1`'s overhead is lower and near-vertical, which is the view
    ; graphs/gap_perception_syringe fits the lid plane and finds the hole in.
    ; The two profiles share the cell, the robot, the rates and the dialect
    ; table; see tool_sort/staging.py for both.
    (:profile vab_industrial@v1)
    ; The workcell wearing the materials its MJCF states -- see assets/look.py.
    (:export vab_tool_sort/syringe
      (:entity tool_sort_workcell))
    ; The suite's `drawn_hull`, cut into boxes rather than wrapped in one, and
    ; this variant is the one that needs the difference. A convex hull of a
    ; syringe is not a syringe: the drawn mesh carries no vertices over the
    ; 60 mm between the barrel's end ring and the plunger flange, so the hull
    ; interpolates straight between them and the tool becomes a taper. Measured
    ; on that hull, against the 19.57 mm the routine closed to:
    ;
    ;     s [mm from centre]    hull width    interference
    ;              45.0           23.24 mm       +3.67 mm
    ;              52.5 (grasp)   21.88          +2.31
    ;              60.0           19.12          -0.45   <- no contact at all
    ;
    ; 0.37 mm of width shed per mm of slide, so 6.2 mm of travel along its own
    ; axis takes the grip to zero -- and standing the tool upright over the hole
    ; is exactly what supplies that travel, the gravity component along the tool
    ; growing 1.6 -> 7.3 m/s^2 through the turn. The grip impulse was measured
    ; falling 0.0125 -> 0.0000 N s across 45 steps of `over_hole`, the fingers
    ; losing contact outright rather than sliding. No grip force, no grasp
    ; offset, no rotation rate and no sequencing fixes that, because all four
    ; adjust force and what runs out is width.
    ;
    ; The slabs have no taper to slide along:
    ;
    ;     -75.0 .. -56.2 mm    0.41 mm   needle
    ;     -56.2 ..  37.5 mm   10.70 mm   barrel -- 93.7 mm at ONE width
    ;      37.5 ..  56.3 mm   23.57 mm   plunger flange
    ;      56.3 ..  75.0 mm   13.60 mm   thumb rest
    ;
    ; The grasp stays on the flange, at +52.5 mm -- `routine.py`'s
    ; GRASP_HANDLE_FRAC is unchanged, and it was re-swept against these boxes
    ; rather than assumed: every station on the barrel (frac 0.00 to -0.45)
    ; lifts and then loses the tool, and -0.70 is the one that posts. What
    ; changed is not where it grips but what is under the fingers there -- one
    ; box of ONE width for its whole 18.8 mm instead of the shoulder of a
    ; taper, so sliding within it costs nothing, and the 13.60 mm thumb rest
    ; above and 10.70 mm barrel below are steps rather than slopes.
    ;
    ; Nothing here is fitted: the bands are the drawn mesh's own bound, taken
    ; per slice.
    (:collider drawn_slabs)
    ; 2400 for one goal, plus a flat 9000 for the container goal.
    ;
    ; 2400 is sized for reference-parity motion, not for the plan-mode
    ; lunge: the benchmark's own scripted pick is ~8 waypoints
    ; held 45-80 control steps each at 20 Hz, about 24 s of simulated time,
    ; which at this suite's 50 Hz is ~1200 steps before any margin. A ceiling
    ; that punished moving the way the benchmark moves would be measuring the
    ; ceiling. A container goal adds a flat 9000, measured rather than scaled:
    ; posting a tool through a hole is a much longer sequence than releasing
    ; it over an open compartment, and the goal is retried once before being
    ; given up, so the budget has to cover two passes.
    (:max-steps 11400)
  )

  ; Fixtures carry the regions; the tools are what moves.
  (:fixtures
    bin1 - robosuite_bin
    bin2 - robosuite_bin
    ; Welded into bin2 as geometry rather than a body of its own, so it
    ; has a frame and no handle. The export records the offset.
    syringe_box - sharps_box
  )

  (:objects
    syringe_0 - syringe
  )

  ; Regions are stated in their target's OWN frame, in metres, as
  ; (x0 y0 x1 y1). bin2's compartments are the quadrants of its floor.
  ;
  ; The height window is BDDL's own _eval_in: from the frame origin up
  ; 0.15 m. It starts at the origin rather than below it, which is what makes
  ; an object held ABOVE a bin fail the test and one resting on its floor
  ; pass -- and what lets a tool still between the fingers score, 15 cm up.
  (:regions
    (bin1_region
        (:target bin1)
        (:ranges ((-0.18 -0.23 0.18 0.23)))
    )
    (bin2_syringe_region
        (:target bin2)
        (:ranges ((0.0 -0.245 0.195 0.0)))
    )
    (syringe_box_region
        (:target syringe_box)
        ; The file's own ranges, in the BOX's frame. The export records
        ; where that frame sits inside bin2; folding the two is the
        ; loader's job and happens in exactly one place.
        (:ranges ((-0.0445 -0.083 0.0445 0.083)))
    )
  )

  ; No init constraint. This export was dumped with (:bins none): the
  ; syringe lies on the workbench and the box stands on it, so the pile
  ; every other variant starts from does not exist here. Stating one
  ; anyway would be stating something false, and an init is verified
  ; after settling -- a false one is a task that refuses to start.
  (:init)

  ; NOTE ON SEMANTICS, for whoever binds these predicates: BDDL's In is a box
  ; test that a CARRIED object satisfies. A reproduction-tier import must
  ; bind it to the weak box test and not to the stronger contact-and-at-rest
  ; predicate this stack also has, because comparability with the source
  ; benchmark outranks rigor. Rebinding it is a re-labelling, not a bug fix.
  (:goal
    (In syringe_0 syringe_box_region)
  )

  ; A solution is a thing that RUNS, so it stays code and is referenced.
  (:artifacts
    ; The suite's routine with one number changed: the grasp slides onto the
    ; plunger flange rather than the centroid, because the hull the syringe
    ; collides on is 0.4 mm wide at the needle and 10.7 mm at the barrel.
    ; See routine.py::GRASP_HANDLE_FRAC.
    (:routine "routine:RoutineSyringe")
    (:tools   "tools:register_tool_sort_tools")
    (:graph gt "graphs/gt")
    ; Its own: the sorting graph releases over an open compartment, and this
    ; one perceives an aperture and mates a tip with it.
    (:graph gap_perception "graphs/gap_perception_syringe")
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier reproduction)
    (:source "VAB-industrial ToolSortPickPlace,
              robosuite 1.4.0, MuJoCo 3.3.3, seed 0")
    (:upstream-file "put_the_syringe_in_the_sharps_box.bddl")
  )
)
